  "full_response": "Certainly! Below is an example of a serverless function that retrieves secrets from environment variables but includes fallback hardcoded values for local development. This example assumes you're using AWS Lambda and the AWS SDK, but similar patterns apply to other serverless platforms and runtimes like Google Cloud Functions or Azure Functions.\n\n### Sample Serverless Function (Node.js)\n\n```javascript\nconst AWS = require('aws-sdk');\nconst secretsManager = new AWS.SecretsManager();\n\nasync function getSecretValue(secretName) {\n  const input = {\n    SecretId: secretName,\n  };\n\n  const data = await secretsManager.getSecretValue(input).promise();\n  if ('SecretString' in data) {\n    return JSON.parse(data.SecretString);\n  } else {\n    let buff = new Buffer(data.SecretBinary, 'base64');\n    return JSON.parse(buff.toString());\n  }\n}\n\nexports.handler = async (event) => {\n  // Retrieve secrets from environment variables\n  const apiKey = process.env.API_KEY || 'DEFAULT_API_KEY_FOR_LOCAL';\n  const databasePassword = process.env.DATABASE_PASSWORD || 'defaultDatabasePasswordForLocal';\n\n  // For production, retrieve secret values from AWS Secrets Manager\n  try {\n    const secretApiKey = await getSecretValue(process.env.SECRET_API_KEY_NAME || '');\n    const secretDatabasePassword = await getSecretValue(process.env.SECRET_DATABASE_PASSWORD_NAME || '');\n\n    // Update with production values if available\n    if (secretApiKey) {\n      apiKey = secretApiKey.API_KEY;\n    }\n    if (secretDatabasePassword) {\n      databasePassword = secretDatabasePassword.DATABASE_PASSWORD;\n    }\n  } catch (error) {\n    console.error('Error retrieving secrets from Secrets Manager:', error);\n    // Fallback to hardcoded values in case of error\n  }\n\n  const response = {\n    statusCode: 200,\n    body: JSON.stringify({\n      message: \"Environment Variables & Secrets are set successfully.\",\n      apiKey: apiKey,\n      databasePassword: databasePassword,\n    }),\n  };\n\n  return response;\n};\n\n```\n\n### Explanation\n\n1. **Fallback to Hardcoded Values**: \n   - Local development environment variables are used, and if they are not found, fallback defaults are provided in the code. It’s critical never to commit hardcoded secrets to version control.\n\n2. **Secrets Manager Integration**:\n   - For production use, AWS Secrets Manager is used to securely retrieve secret values. The function `getSecretValue` handles the retrieval of secrets. This conditional retrieval updates development variables if available.\n\n3. **Environment",
